1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,559
It was a dark night, though the full moon rose as I left the enclosed lands,

2
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:14,438
and passed out upon the marshes. Beyond their dark line there was a ribbon of clear sky,

3
00:00:14,438 --> 00:00:19,597
hardly broad enough to hold the red large moon. In a few minutes she had

4
00:00:19,597 --> 00:00:24,879
ascended out of that clear field, in among the piled mountains of cloud.

5
00:00:24,879 --> 00:00:30,319
There was a melancholy wind, and the marshes were very dismal. A stranger would have found

6
00:00:30,319 --> 00:00:36,160
them insupportable, and even to me they were so oppressive that I hesitated, half inclined to go

7
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:42,879
back. But I knew them well, and could have found my way on a far darker night, and had no excuse

8
00:00:42,879 --> 00:00:51,237
for returning, being there. So, having come there against my inclination, I went on against it.

9
00:00:51,237 --> 00:00:56,796
The direction that I took was not that in which my old home lay, nor that in which we had pursued the

10
00:00:56,796 --> 00:01:03,398
convicts. My back was turned towards the distant Hulks as I walked on, and, though I could see the

11
00:01:03,398 --> 00:01:09,597
old lights away on the spits of sand, I saw them over my shoulder. I knew the limekiln as well as

12
00:01:09,597 --> 00:01:15,559
I knew the old Battery, but they were miles apart; so that, if a light had been burning at each point

13
00:01:15,559 --> 00:01:21,680
that night, there would have been a long strip of the blank horizon between the two bright specks.

14
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:26,519
At first, I had to shut some gates after me, and now and then to stand still while

15
00:01:26,519 --> 00:01:30,718
the cattle that were lying in the banked-up pathway arose and blundered down among the

16
00:01:30,718 --> 00:01:37,637
grass and reeds. But after a little while I seemed to have the whole flats to myself.

17
00:01:37,637 --> 00:01:42,758
It was another half-hour before I drew near to the kiln. The lime was burning

18
00:01:42,758 --> 00:01:48,519
with a sluggish stifling smell, but the fires were made up and left,

19
00:01:48,519 --> 00:01:56,078
and no workmen were visible. Hard by was a small stone-quarry. It lay directly in my way,

20
00:01:56,078 --> 00:02:01,796
and had been worked that day, as I saw by the tools and barrows that were lying about.

21
00:02:01,796 --> 00:02:07,277
Coming up again to the marsh level out of this excavation,—for the rude path lay through it,—I

22
00:02:07,277 --> 00:02:13,597
saw a light in the old sluice-house. I quickened my pace, and knocked at the door with my hand.

23
00:02:13,597 --> 00:02:19,239
Waiting for some reply, I looked about me, noticing how the sluice was abandoned and broken,

24
00:02:20,038 --> 00:02:25,917
and how the house—of wood with a tiled roof—would not be proof against the weather much longer,

25
00:02:25,917 --> 00:02:30,758
if it were so even now, and how the mud and ooze were coated with lime,

26
00:02:30,758 --> 00:02:37,000
and how the choking vapour of the kiln crept in a ghostly way towards me. Still there was no answer,

27
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,637
and I knocked again. No answer still, and I tried the latch.

28
00:02:42,637 --> 00:02:48,680
It rose under my hand, and the door yielded. Looking in, I saw a lighted candle on a table,

29
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:54,758
a bench, and a mattress on a truckle bedstead. As there was a loft above,

30
00:02:54,758 --> 00:03:02,078
I called, “Is there any one here?” but no voice answered. Then I looked at my watch,

31
00:03:02,078 --> 00:03:08,436
and, finding that it was past nine, called again, “Is there any one here?” There being

32
00:03:08,438 --> 00:03:12,680
still no answer, I went out at the door, irresolute what to do.

33
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:18,078
It was beginning to rain fast. Seeing nothing save what I had seen already,

34
00:03:18,078 --> 00:03:22,237
I turned back into the house, and stood just within the shelter of the doorway,

35
00:03:22,237 --> 00:03:26,597
looking out into the night. While I was considering that some one must have been

36
00:03:26,597 --> 00:03:32,917
there lately and must soon be coming back, or the candle would not be burning, it came into my head

37
00:03:32,917 --> 00:03:40,160
to look if the wick were long. I turned round to do so, and had taken up the candle in my hand,

38
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:46,078
when it was extinguished by some violent shock; and the next thing I comprehended was,

39
00:03:46,078 --> 00:03:52,237
that I had been caught in a strong running noose, thrown over my head from behind.

40
00:03:52,237 --> 00:03:56,879
“Now,” said a suppressed voice with an oath, “I’ve got you!”

41
00:03:56,879 --> 00:04:02,796
“What is this?” I cried, struggling. “Who is it? Help, help, help!”

42
00:04:02,796 --> 00:04:07,358
Not only were my arms pulled close to my sides, but the pressure on my bad arm

43
00:04:07,358 --> 00:04:14,436
caused me exquisite pain. Sometimes, a strong man’s hand, sometimes a strong man’s breast,

44
00:04:14,438 --> 00:04:20,480
was set against my mouth to deaden my cries, and with a hot breath always close to me,

45
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:27,635
I struggled ineffectually in the dark, while I was fastened tight to the wall. “And now,” said

46
00:04:27,637 --> 00:04:34,199
the suppressed voice with another oath, “call out again, and I’ll make short work of you!”

47
00:04:34,199 --> 00:04:39,237
Faint and sick with the pain of my injured arm, bewildered by the surprise,

48
00:04:39,237 --> 00:04:44,519
and yet conscious how easily this threat could be put in execution, I desisted,

49
00:04:44,519 --> 00:04:49,160
and tried to ease my arm were it ever so little. But, it was bound too tight

50
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:56,116
for that. I felt as if, having been burnt before, it were now being boiled.

51
00:04:56,116 --> 00:05:01,038
The sudden exclusion of the night, and the substitution of black darkness in its place,

52
00:05:01,038 --> 00:05:06,480
warned me that the man had closed a shutter. After groping about for a little,

53
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:12,756
he found the flint and steel he wanted, and began to strike a light. I strained my sight upon the

54
00:05:12,758 --> 00:05:19,199
sparks that fell among the tinder, and upon which he breathed and breathed, match in hand,

55
00:05:19,199 --> 00:05:25,519
but I could only see his lips, and the blue point of the match; even those but fitfully.

56
00:05:25,519 --> 00:05:33,160
The tinder was damp,—no wonder there,—and one after another the sparks died out.

57
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:39,116
The man was in no hurry, and struck again with the flint and steel. As the sparks fell thick

58
00:05:39,116 --> 00:05:45,116
and bright about him, I could see his hands, and touches of his face, and could make out

59
00:05:45,116 --> 00:05:52,000
that he was seated and bending over the table; but nothing more. Presently I saw his blue lips again,

60
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,718
breathing on the tinder, and then a flare of light flashed up, and showed me Orlick.

61
00:05:56,718 --> 00:06:02,480
Whom I had looked for, I don’t know. I had not looked for him. Seeing him,

62
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:08,358
I felt that I was in a dangerous strait indeed, and I kept my eyes upon him.

63
00:06:08,358 --> 00:06:13,358
He lighted the candle from the flaring match with great deliberation, and dropped the match,

64
00:06:13,358 --> 00:06:17,358
and trod it out. Then he put the candle away from him on the table,

65
00:06:17,358 --> 00:06:21,398
so that he could see me, and sat with his arms folded on the table and looked

66
00:06:21,398 --> 00:06:26,480
at me. I made out that I was fastened to a stout perpendicular ladder a few inches

67
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:31,718
from the wall,—a fixture there,—the means of ascent to the loft above.

68
00:06:31,718 --> 00:06:36,957
“Now,” said he, when we had surveyed one another for some time, “I’ve got you.”

69
00:06:36,957 --> 00:06:39,597
“Unbind me. Let me go!”

70
00:06:39,597 --> 00:06:44,237
“Ah!” he returned, “I’ll let you go. I’ll let you go to the moon,

71
00:06:44,237 --> 00:06:48,199
I’ll let you go to the stars. All in good time.”

72
00:06:48,199 --> 00:06:50,480
“Why have you lured me here?”

73
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:54,038
“Don’t you know?” said he, with a deadly look.

74
00:06:54,038 --> 00:06:56,758
“Why have you set upon me in the dark?”

75
00:06:56,758 --> 00:07:05,358
“Because I mean to do it all myself. One keeps a secret better than two. O you enemy, you enemy!”

76
00:07:05,358 --> 00:07:11,199
His enjoyment of the spectacle I furnished, as he sat with his arms folded on the table, shaking his

77
00:07:11,199 --> 00:07:17,678
head at me and hugging himself, had a malignity in it that made me tremble. As I watched him in

78
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:25,199
silence, he put his hand into the corner at his side, and took up a gun with a brass-bound stock.

79
00:07:25,199 --> 00:07:29,160
“Do you know this?” said he, making as if he would take aim at me. “Do

80
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:32,557
you know where you saw it afore? Speak, wolf!”

81
00:07:32,559 --> 00:07:33,680
“Yes,” I answered.

82
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:37,637
“You cost me that place. You did. Speak!”

83
00:07:37,637 --> 00:07:39,758
“What else could I do?”

84
00:07:39,758 --> 00:07:41,957
“You did that, and that would be enough,

85
00:07:41,957 --> 00:07:47,519
without more. How dared you to come betwixt me and a young woman I liked?”

86
00:07:47,519 --> 00:07:49,199
“When did I?”

87
00:07:49,199 --> 00:07:54,519
“When didn’t you? It was you as always give Old Orlick a bad name to her.”

88
00:07:54,519 --> 00:07:58,758
“You gave it to yourself; you gained it for yourself. I could have done you no harm,

89
00:07:58,758 --> 00:08:01,038
if you had done yourself none.”

90
00:08:01,038 --> 00:08:06,597
“You’re a liar. And you’ll take any pains, and spend any money, to drive me out of this country,

91
00:08:06,597 --> 00:08:13,398
will you?” said he, repeating my words to Biddy in the last interview I had with her. “Now, I’ll tell

92
00:08:13,398 --> 00:08:18,957
you a piece of information. It was never so well worth your while to get me out of this country as

93
00:08:18,957 --> 00:08:27,680
it is to-night. Ah! If it was all your money twenty times told, to the last brass farden!”

94
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:34,559
As he shook his heavy hand at me, with his mouth snarling like a tiger’s, I felt that it was true.

95
00:08:34,559 --> 00:08:36,879
“What are you going to do to me?”

96
00:08:36,879 --> 00:08:41,839
“I’m a-going,” said he, bringing his fist down upon the table with a heavy blow,

97
00:08:41,839 --> 00:08:48,237
and rising as the blow fell to give it greater force,—“I’m a-going to have your life!”

98
00:08:48,237 --> 00:08:52,758
He leaned forward staring at me, slowly unclenched his hand and drew

99
00:08:52,758 --> 00:08:58,237
it across his mouth as if his mouth watered for me, and sat down again.

100
00:08:58,237 --> 00:09:03,116
“You was always in Old Orlick’s way since ever you was a child.

101
00:09:03,116 --> 00:09:10,000
You goes out of his way this present night. He’ll have no more on you. You’re dead.”

102
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,480
I felt that I had come to the brink of my grave. For a moment

103
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:18,756
I looked wildly round my trap for any chance of escape; but there was none.

104
00:09:20,358 --> 00:09:27,160
“More than that,” said he, folding his arms on the table again, “I won’t have a rag of you,

105
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:33,277
I won’t have a bone of you, left on earth. I’ll put your body in the kiln,—I’d carry two such to

106
00:09:33,277 --> 00:09:40,000
it, on my shoulders—and, let people suppose what they may of you, they shall never know nothing.”

107
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,637
My mind, with inconceivable rapidity followed out all the consequences

108
00:09:44,637 --> 00:09:48,718
of such a death. Estella’s father would believe I had deserted him,

109
00:09:49,277 --> 00:09:55,160
would be taken, would die accusing me; even Herbert would doubt me, when he compared the

110
00:09:55,160 --> 00:10:00,917
letter I had left for him with the fact that I had called at Miss Havisham’s gate for only a moment;

111
00:10:00,917 --> 00:10:06,237
Joe and Biddy would never know how sorry I had been that night, none would ever know what I

112
00:10:06,237 --> 00:10:14,796
had suffered, how true I had meant to be, what an agony I had passed through. The death close

113
00:10:14,796 --> 00:10:21,078
before me was terrible, but far more terrible than death was the dread of being misremembered after

114
00:10:21,078 --> 00:10:29,398
death. And so quick were my thoughts, that I saw myself despised by unborn generations,—Estella’s

115
00:10:29,398 --> 00:10:35,237
children, and their children,—while the wretch’s words were yet on his lips.

116
00:10:35,237 --> 00:10:41,839
“Now, wolf,” said he, “afore I kill you like any other beast,—which is wot I mean to do and wot I

117
00:10:41,839 --> 00:10:48,637
have tied you up for,—I’ll have a good look at you and a good goad at you. O you enemy!”

118
00:10:51,078 --> 00:10:56,957
It had passed through my thoughts to cry out for help again; though few could know better than I,

119
00:10:56,957 --> 00:11:03,038
the solitary nature of the spot, and the hopelessness of aid. But as he sat gloating

120
00:11:03,038 --> 00:11:10,758
over me, I was supported by a scornful detestation of him that sealed my lips. Above all things,

121
00:11:10,758 --> 00:11:16,718
I resolved that I would not entreat him, and that I would die making some last poor resistance to

122
00:11:16,718 --> 00:11:23,160
him. Softened as my thoughts of all the rest of men were in that dire extremity;

123
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:29,718
humbly beseeching pardon, as I did, of Heaven; melted at heart, as I was, by the thought that

124
00:11:29,718 --> 00:11:35,398
I had taken no farewell, and never now could take farewell of those who were dear to me,

125
00:11:35,398 --> 00:11:41,756
or could explain myself to them, or ask for their compassion on my miserable errors,—still,

126
00:11:41,758 --> 00:11:47,000
if I could have killed him, even in dying, I would have done it.

127
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:53,680
He had been drinking, and his eyes were red and bloodshot. Around his neck was slung a tin bottle,

128
00:11:53,680 --> 00:12:00,319
as I had often seen his meat and drink slung about him in other days. He brought the bottle to his

129
00:12:00,319 --> 00:12:08,398
lips, and took a fiery drink from it; and I smelt the strong spirits that I saw flash into his face.

130
00:12:08,398 --> 00:12:12,038
“Wolf!” said he, folding his arms again,

131
00:12:12,038 --> 00:12:18,597
“Old Orlick’s a-going to tell you somethink. It was you as did for your shrew sister.”

132
00:12:18,597 --> 00:12:22,637
Again my mind, with its former inconceivable rapidity,

133
00:12:22,637 --> 00:12:28,839
had exhausted the whole subject of the attack upon my sister, her illness, and her death,

134
00:12:28,839 --> 00:12:34,319
before his slow and hesitating speech had formed these words.

135
00:12:34,319 --> 00:12:37,000
“It was you, villain,” said I.

136
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,078
“I tell you it was your doing,—I tell you it was done through you,” he retorted,

137
00:12:42,078 --> 00:12:47,680
catching up the gun, and making a blow with the stock at the vacant air between us. “I

138
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:54,277
come upon her from behind, as I come upon you to-night. I giv’ it her! I left her for dead,

139
00:12:54,277 --> 00:12:59,480
and if there had been a limekiln as nigh her as there is now nigh you, she shouldn’t have come to

140
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:07,078
life again. But it warn’t Old Orlick as did it; it was you. You was favoured, and he was bullied and

141
00:13:07,078 --> 00:13:14,358
beat. Old Orlick bullied and beat, eh? Now you pays for it. You done it; now you pays for it.”

142
00:13:14,358 --> 00:13:20,557
He drank again, and became more ferocious. I saw by his tilting of the bottle that there was no

143
00:13:20,559 --> 00:13:26,160
great quantity left in it. I distinctly understood that he was working himself up with its contents

144
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:33,597
to make an end of me. I knew that every drop it held was a drop of my life. I knew that when I

145
00:13:33,597 --> 00:13:39,116
was changed into a part of the vapour that had crept towards me but a little while before, like

146
00:13:39,116 --> 00:13:45,680
my own warning ghost, he would do as he had done in my sister’s case,—make all haste to the town,

147
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:52,718
and be seen slouching about there drinking at the alehouses. My rapid mind pursued him to the town,

148
00:13:52,718 --> 00:13:57,438
made a picture of the street with him in it, and contrasted its lights and life with the

149
00:13:57,438 --> 00:14:02,718
lonely marsh and the white vapour creeping over it, into which I should have dissolved.

150
00:14:02,718 --> 00:14:08,358
It was not only that I could have summed up years and years and years while he said a dozen words,

151
00:14:08,358 --> 00:14:14,480
but that what he did say presented pictures to me, and not mere words. In the excited

152
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:18,436
and exalted state of my brain, I could not think of a place without seeing it,

153
00:14:19,319 --> 00:14:25,000
or of persons without seeing them. It is impossible to overstate the vividness of

154
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:31,358
these images, and yet I was so intent, all the time, upon him himself,—who would not be

155
00:14:31,358 --> 00:14:39,038
intent on the tiger crouching to spring!—that I knew of the slightest action of his fingers.

156
00:14:39,038 --> 00:14:43,436
When he had drunk this second time, he rose from the bench on which he sat,

157
00:14:43,438 --> 00:14:49,519
and pushed the table aside. Then, he took up the candle, and, shading it with his murderous hand

158
00:14:49,519 --> 00:14:56,839
so as to throw its light on me, stood before me, looking at me and enjoying the sight.

159
00:14:56,839 --> 00:15:00,237
“Wolf, I’ll tell you something more. It was Old

160
00:15:00,237 --> 00:15:03,758
Orlick as you tumbled over on your stairs that night.”

161
00:15:03,758 --> 00:15:09,078
I saw the staircase with its extinguished lamps. I saw the shadows of the heavy stair-rails,

162
00:15:09,078 --> 00:15:15,480
thrown by the watchman’s lantern on the wall. I saw the rooms that I was never to see again; here,

163
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:22,597
a door half open; there, a door closed; all the articles of furniture around.

164
00:15:22,597 --> 00:15:28,277
“And why was Old Orlick there? I’ll tell you something more, wolf. You and her have

165
00:15:28,277 --> 00:15:33,436
pretty well hunted me out of this country, so far as getting a easy living in it goes,

166
00:15:33,438 --> 00:15:38,758
and I’ve took up with new companions, and new masters. Some of ’em writes my letters

167
00:15:38,758 --> 00:15:45,480
when I wants ’em wrote,—do you mind?—writes my letters, wolf! They writes fifty hands;

168
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:50,678
they’re not like sneaking you, as writes but one. I’ve had a firm mind and a firm will to have your

169
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:56,879
life, since you was down here at your sister’s burying. I han’t seen a way to get you safe,

170
00:15:56,879 --> 00:16:02,160
and I’ve looked arter you to know your ins and outs. For, says Old Orlick to himself,

171
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:08,277
‘Somehow or another I’ll have him!’ What! When I looks for you, I finds your uncle Provis, eh?”

172
00:16:08,277 --> 00:16:13,756
Mill Pond Bank, and Millstone Basin, and the Old Green Copper Rope-walk,

173
00:16:13,758 --> 00:16:20,160
all so clear and plain! Provis in his rooms, the signal whose use was over, pretty Clara,

174
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:25,637
the good motherly woman, old Bill Barley on his back, all drifting by, as on the swift

175
00:16:25,637 --> 00:16:32,680
stream of my life fast running out to sea! “You with a uncle too! Why, I know’d you

176
00:16:32,680 --> 00:16:37,358
at Gargery’s when you was so small a wolf that I could have took your weazen betwixt

177
00:16:37,358 --> 00:16:42,116
this finger and thumb and chucked you away dead (as I’d thoughts o’ doing, odd times,

178
00:16:42,116 --> 00:16:48,237
when I see you loitering amongst the pollards on a Sunday), and you hadn’t found no uncles then.

179
00:16:49,199 --> 00:16:55,237
No, not you! But when Old Orlick come for to hear that your uncle Provis had most like wore

180
00:16:55,237 --> 00:17:00,480
the leg-iron wot Old Orlick had picked up, filed asunder, on these meshes ever so many year ago,

181
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:04,519
and wot he kep by him till he dropped your sister with it, like a bullock, as he means to

182
00:17:04,519 --> 00:17:11,839
drop you—hey?—when he come for to hear that—hey?” In his savage taunting, he flared the candle so

183
00:17:11,839 --> 00:17:16,879
close at me that I turned my face aside to save it from the flame.

184
00:17:16,879 --> 00:17:24,358
“Ah!” he cried, laughing, after doing it again, “the burnt child dreads the fire! Old Orlick

185
00:17:24,358 --> 00:17:29,796
knowed you was burnt, Old Orlick knowed you was smuggling your uncle Provis away,

186
00:17:29,796 --> 00:17:36,199
Old Orlick’s a match for you and know’d you’d come to-night! Now I’ll tell you something more, wolf,

187
00:17:36,199 --> 00:17:42,398
and this ends it. There’s them that’s as good a match for your uncle Provis as Old Orlick has

188
00:17:42,398 --> 00:17:48,678
been for you. Let him ’ware them, when he’s lost his nevvy! Let him ’ware them, when no man can’t

189
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:55,519
find a rag of his dear relation’s clothes, nor yet a bone of his body. There’s them that can’t

190
00:17:55,519 --> 00:18:00,680
and that won’t have Magwitch,—yes, I know the name!—alive in the same land with them,

191
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:06,237
and that’s had such sure information of him when he was alive in another land, as that he couldn’t

192
00:18:06,237 --> 00:18:12,358
and shouldn’t leave it unbeknown and put them in danger. P’raps it’s them that writes fifty hands,

193
00:18:12,358 --> 00:18:19,756
and that’s not like sneaking you as writes but one. ’Ware Compeyson, Magwitch, and the gallows!”

194
00:18:19,758 --> 00:18:25,758
He flared the candle at me again, smoking my face and hair, and for an instant blinding me,

195
00:18:25,758 --> 00:18:32,160
and turned his powerful back as he replaced the light on the table. I had thought a prayer,

196
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:36,839
and had been with Joe and Biddy and Herbert, before he turned towards me again.

197
00:18:36,839 --> 00:18:43,358
There was a clear space of a few feet between the table and the opposite wall. Within this space,

198
00:18:43,358 --> 00:18:49,237
he now slouched backwards and forwards. His great strength seemed to sit stronger upon

199
00:18:49,237 --> 00:18:55,160
him than ever before, as he did this with his hands hanging loose and heavy at his sides,

200
00:18:55,160 --> 00:19:03,358
and with his eyes scowling at me. I had no grain of hope left. Wild as my inward hurry was,

201
00:19:03,358 --> 00:19:07,838
and wonderful the force of the pictures that rushed by me instead of thoughts,

202
00:19:07,839 --> 00:19:13,319
I could yet clearly understand that, unless he had resolved that I was within a few moments of

203
00:19:13,319 --> 00:19:20,078
surely perishing out of all human knowledge, he would never have told me what he had told.

204
00:19:20,078 --> 00:19:27,796
Of a sudden, he stopped, took the cork out of his bottle, and tossed it away. Light as it was,

205
00:19:27,796 --> 00:19:34,277
I heard it fall like a plummet. He swallowed slowly, tilting up the bottle by little and

206
00:19:34,277 --> 00:19:41,480
little, and now he looked at me no more. The last few drops of liquor he poured into the palm of his

207
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:48,838
hand, and licked up. Then, with a sudden hurry of violence and swearing horribly, he threw the

208
00:19:48,839 --> 00:19:56,597
bottle from him, and stooped; and I saw in his hand a stone-hammer with a long heavy handle.

209
00:19:56,597 --> 00:20:02,237
The resolution I had made did not desert me, for, without uttering one vain word of appeal to him,

210
00:20:02,237 --> 00:20:08,000
I shouted out with all my might, and struggled with all my might. It was only my head and

211
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:13,718
my legs that I could move, but to that extent I struggled with all the force, until then unknown,

212
00:20:13,718 --> 00:20:19,957
that was within me. In the same instant I heard responsive shouts, saw figures and a gleam of

213
00:20:19,957 --> 00:20:25,116
light dash in at the door, heard voices and tumult, and saw Orlick emerge from a struggle

214
00:20:25,116 --> 00:20:32,038
of men, as if it were tumbling water, clear the table at a leap, and fly out into the night.

215
00:20:32,038 --> 00:20:36,796
After a blank, I found that I was lying unbound, on the floor, in the same place,

216
00:20:36,796 --> 00:20:42,160
with my head on some one’s knee. My eyes were fixed on the ladder against the wall,

217
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:48,237
when I came to myself,—had opened on it before my mind saw it,—and thus as I recovered

218
00:20:48,237 --> 00:20:53,519
consciousness, I knew that I was in the place where I had lost it.

219
00:20:53,519 --> 00:20:58,839
Too indifferent at first, even to look round and ascertain who supported me,

220
00:20:58,839 --> 00:21:07,718
I was lying looking at the ladder, when there came between me and it a face. The face of Trabb’s boy!

221
00:21:07,718 --> 00:21:14,438
“I think he’s all right!” said Trabb’s boy, in a sober voice; “but ain’t he just pale though!”

222
00:21:14,438 --> 00:21:18,879
At these words, the face of him who supported me looked over into mine,

223
00:21:18,879 --> 00:21:21,398
and I saw my supporter to be—

224
00:21:21,398 --> 00:21:24,237
“Herbert! Great Heaven!”

225
00:21:24,237 --> 00:21:29,319
“Softly,” said Herbert. “Gently, Handel. Don’t be too eager.”

226
00:21:29,319 --> 00:21:34,796
“And our old comrade, Startop!” I cried, as he too bent over me.

227
00:21:34,796 --> 00:21:40,519
“Remember what he is going to assist us in,” said Herbert, “and be calm.”

228
00:21:40,519 --> 00:21:46,718
The allusion made me spring up; though I dropped again from the pain in my arm.

229
00:21:46,718 --> 00:21:51,237
“The time has not gone by, Herbert, has it? What night is to-night? How

230
00:21:51,237 --> 00:21:55,519
long have I been here?” For, I had a strange and strong misgiving that

231
00:21:55,519 --> 00:22:02,038
I had been lying there a long time—a day and a night,—two days and nights,—more.

232
00:22:02,038 --> 00:22:06,637
“The time has not gone by. It is still Monday night.”

233
00:22:06,637 --> 00:22:09,796
“Thank God!” “And you have all

234
00:22:09,796 --> 00:22:14,277
to-morrow, Tuesday, to rest in,” said Herbert. “But you can’t help groaning,

235
00:22:14,277 --> 00:22:18,319
my dear Handel. What hurt have you got? Can you stand?”

236
00:22:18,319 --> 00:22:24,237
“Yes, yes,” said I, “I can walk. I have no hurt but in this throbbing arm.”

237
00:22:24,237 --> 00:22:30,038
They laid it bare, and did what they could. It was violently swollen and inflamed, and I could

238
00:22:30,038 --> 00:22:35,680
scarcely endure to have it touched. But, they tore up their handkerchiefs to make fresh bandages,

239
00:22:35,680 --> 00:22:41,160
and carefully replaced it in the sling, until we could get to the town and obtain some cooling

240
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:47,879
lotion to put upon it. In a little while we had shut the door of the dark and empty sluice-house,

241
00:22:47,879 --> 00:22:53,718
and were passing through the quarry on our way back. Trabb’s boy—Trabb’s overgrown young man

242
00:22:53,718 --> 00:23:00,000
now—went before us with a lantern, which was the light I had seen come in at the door. But,

243
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:04,917
the moon was a good two hours higher than when I had last seen the sky, and the night,

244
00:23:04,917 --> 00:23:09,758
though rainy, was much lighter. The white vapour of the kiln was passing

245
00:23:09,758 --> 00:23:16,438
from us as we went by, and as I had thought a prayer before, I thought a thanksgiving now.

246
00:23:16,438 --> 00:23:22,038
Entreating Herbert to tell me how he had come to my rescue,—which at first he had flatly refused

247
00:23:22,038 --> 00:23:29,398
to do, but had insisted on my remaining quiet,—I learnt that I had in my hurry dropped the letter,

248
00:23:29,398 --> 00:23:35,480
open, in our chambers, where he, coming home to bring with him Startop whom he had met in

249
00:23:35,480 --> 00:23:42,718
the street on his way to me, found it, very soon after I was gone. Its tone made him uneasy, and

250
00:23:42,718 --> 00:23:47,839
the more so because of the inconsistency between it and the hasty letter I had left for him.

251
00:23:48,559 --> 00:23:53,160
His uneasiness increasing instead of subsiding, after a quarter of an hour’s consideration,

252
00:23:53,160 --> 00:23:58,398
he set off for the coach-office with Startop, who volunteered his company, to make inquiry when the

253
00:23:58,398 --> 00:24:04,398
next coach went down. Finding that the afternoon coach was gone, and finding that his uneasiness

254
00:24:04,398 --> 00:24:10,718
grew into positive alarm, as obstacles came in his way, he resolved to follow in a post-chaise.

255
00:24:10,718 --> 00:24:18,000
So he and Startop arrived at the Blue Boar, fully expecting there to find me, or tidings of me; but,

256
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:24,000
finding neither, went on to Miss Havisham’s, where they lost me. Hereupon they went back to the hotel

257
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:28,559
(doubtless at about the time when I was hearing the popular local version of my own story) to

258
00:24:28,559 --> 00:24:33,680
refresh themselves and to get some one to guide them out upon the marshes. Among the loungers

259
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:39,559
under the Boar’s archway happened to be Trabb’s Boy,—true to his ancient habit of happening to be

260
00:24:39,559 --> 00:24:44,758
everywhere where he had no business,—and Trabb’s boy had seen me passing from Miss Havisham’s in

261
00:24:44,758 --> 00:24:50,796
the direction of my dining-place. Thus Trabb’s boy became their guide, and with him they went

262
00:24:50,796 --> 00:24:57,000
out to the sluice-house, though by the town way to the marshes, which I had avoided. Now,

263
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:02,519
as they went along, Herbert reflected, that I might, after all, have been brought there on some

264
00:25:02,519 --> 00:25:08,438
genuine and serviceable errand tending to Provis’s safety, and, bethinking himself that in that case

265
00:25:08,438 --> 00:25:14,519
interruption must be mischievous, left his guide and Startop on the edge of the quarry, and went

266
00:25:14,519 --> 00:25:20,758
on by himself, and stole round the house two or three times, endeavouring to ascertain whether

267
00:25:20,758 --> 00:25:28,358
all was right within. As he could hear nothing but indistinct sounds of one deep rough voice (this

268
00:25:28,358 --> 00:25:35,038
was while my mind was so busy), he even at last began to doubt whether I was there, when suddenly

269
00:25:35,038 --> 00:25:42,637
I cried out loudly, and he answered the cries, and rushed in, closely followed by the other two.

270
00:25:42,637 --> 00:25:47,398
When I told Herbert what had passed within the house, he was for our immediately going before

271
00:25:47,398 --> 00:25:54,000
a magistrate in the town, late at night as it was, and getting out a warrant. But,

272
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:00,917
I had already considered that such a course, by detaining us there, or binding us to come back,

273
00:26:00,917 --> 00:26:07,718
might be fatal to Provis. There was no gainsaying this difficulty, and we relinquished all thoughts

274
00:26:07,718 --> 00:26:13,559
of pursuing Orlick at that time. For the present, under the circumstances,

275
00:26:13,559 --> 00:26:18,879
we deemed it prudent to make rather light of the matter to Trabb’s boy; who, I am convinced,

276
00:26:18,879 --> 00:26:23,758
would have been much affected by disappointment, if he had known that his intervention saved me

277
00:26:23,758 --> 00:26:29,519
from the limekiln. Not that Trabb’s boy was of a malignant nature, but that he had too much spare

278
00:26:29,519 --> 00:26:35,718
vivacity, and that it was in his constitution to want variety and excitement at anybody’s expense.

279
00:26:35,718 --> 00:26:41,680
When we parted, I presented him with two guineas (which seemed to meet his views), and told him

280
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:47,000
that I was sorry ever to have had an ill opinion of him (which made no impression on him at all).

281
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:52,160
Wednesday being so close upon us, we determined to go back to London that night, three in the

282
00:26:52,160 --> 00:26:57,199
post-chaise; the rather, as we should then be clear away before the night’s adventure began

283
00:26:57,199 --> 00:27:03,597
to be talked of. Herbert got a large bottle of stuff for my arm; and by dint of having this

284
00:27:03,597 --> 00:27:09,438
stuff dropped over it all the night through, I was just able to bear its pain on the journey.

285
00:27:09,438 --> 00:27:16,839
It was daylight when we reached the Temple, and I went at once to bed, and lay in bed all day.

286
00:27:16,839 --> 00:27:22,078
My terror, as I lay there, of falling ill, and being unfitted for to-morrow,

287
00:27:22,078 --> 00:27:28,160
was so besetting, that I wonder it did not disable me of itself. It would have done so,

288
00:27:28,160 --> 00:27:32,917
pretty surely, in conjunction with the mental wear and tear I had suffered,

289
00:27:32,917 --> 00:27:39,358
but for the unnatural strain upon me that to-morrow was. So anxiously looked forward to,

290
00:27:39,358 --> 00:27:46,116
charged with such consequences, its results so impenetrably hidden, though so near.

291
00:27:46,116 --> 00:27:50,680
No precaution could have been more obvious than our refraining from communication with him that

292
00:27:50,680 --> 00:27:58,116
day; yet this again increased my restlessness. I started at every footstep and every sound,

293
00:27:58,116 --> 00:28:05,078
believing that he was discovered and taken, and this was the messenger to tell me so. I persuaded

294
00:28:05,078 --> 00:28:10,319
myself that I knew he was taken; that there was something more upon my mind than a fear or

295
00:28:10,319 --> 00:28:16,796
a presentiment; that the fact had occurred, and I had a mysterious knowledge of it. As the days

296
00:28:16,796 --> 00:28:23,879
wore on, and no ill news came, as the day closed in and darkness fell, my overshadowing dread of

297
00:28:23,879 --> 00:28:31,237
being disabled by illness before to-morrow morning altogether mastered me. My burning arm throbbed,

298
00:28:31,237 --> 00:28:38,839
and my burning head throbbed, and I fancied I was beginning to wander. I counted up to high numbers,

299
00:28:38,839 --> 00:28:45,559
to make sure of myself, and repeated passages that I knew in prose and verse. It happened

300
00:28:45,559 --> 00:28:52,879
sometimes that in the mere escape of a fatigued mind, I dozed for some moments or forgot;

301
00:28:52,879 --> 00:28:59,597
then I would say to myself with a start, “Now it has come, and I am turning delirious!”

302
00:28:59,597 --> 00:29:05,519
They kept me very quiet all day, and kept my arm constantly dressed, and gave me cooling

303
00:29:05,519 --> 00:29:12,358
drinks. Whenever I fell asleep, I awoke with the notion I had had in the sluice-house,

304
00:29:12,358 --> 00:29:17,917
that a long time had elapsed and the opportunity to save him was gone.

305
00:29:17,917 --> 00:29:22,559
About midnight I got out of bed and went to Herbert, with the conviction that I

306
00:29:22,559 --> 00:29:27,438
had been asleep for four-and-twenty hours, and that Wednesday was past.

307
00:29:27,438 --> 00:29:34,480
It was the last self-exhausting effort of my fretfulness, for after that I slept soundly.

308
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:39,436
Wednesday morning was dawning when I looked out of window. The winking lights upon the bridges

309
00:29:39,438 --> 00:29:46,237
were already pale, the coming sun was like a marsh of fire on the horizon. The river,

310
00:29:46,237 --> 00:29:53,237
still dark and mysterious, was spanned by bridges that were turning coldly grey, with here and there

311
00:29:53,237 --> 00:29:59,635
at top a warm touch from the burning in the sky. As I looked along the clustered roofs,

312
00:29:59,637 --> 00:30:05,796
with church-towers and spires shooting into the unusually clear air, the sun rose up,

313
00:30:05,796 --> 00:30:11,277
and a veil seemed to be drawn from the river, and millions of sparkles burst out upon its

314
00:30:11,277 --> 00:30:17,480
waters. From me too, a veil seemed to be drawn, and I felt strong and well.

315
00:30:17,480 --> 00:30:23,277
Herbert lay asleep in his bed, and our old fellow-student lay asleep on the sofa. I

316
00:30:23,277 --> 00:30:29,078
could not dress myself without help; but I made up the fire, which was still burning, and got

317
00:30:29,078 --> 00:30:35,116
some coffee ready for them. In good time they too started up strong and well, and we admitted

318
00:30:35,116 --> 00:30:41,116
the sharp morning air at the windows, and looked at the tide that was still flowing towards us.

319
00:30:41,116 --> 00:30:45,160
“When it turns at nine o’clock,” said Herbert, cheerfully, “look out for us,

320
00:30:45,160 --> 00:30:47,319
and stand ready, you over there at Mill Pond Bank!”
